    of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
    in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights
    to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell
    copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
    furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:

    The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
    copies or substantial portions of the Software.

    THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
    IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
    FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE
    AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER
    LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,
    OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE
    SOFTWARE.

A file can be "original work" under this section for its prose while still containing a verbatim
excerpt covered by section 3 or 4 below — the MIT grant in this section covers the prose and
analysis, not any quoted third-party source sitting inside the same file. Check for a
`**Source excerpt:**` field before assuming a whole file is unencumbered.

2. Wiki-derived material (wiki-citing entries)
------------------------------------------------

Any doc file whose `**Provenance:**` field cites a wiki page — every tier-A entry by definition,
plus the tier-B entries that started from a wiki page without reaching tier-A's verification bar
— is derived from a saved page on one of two wikis. Those source wikis publish their own content
under terms this repo does not have the authority to override, regardless of how much the doc
file's prose has been rewritten and re-verified against engine source:

- Files whose `Provenance:` cites the ZDoom Wiki (`zdoom.org/wiki`, whether by page title or URL)
  are derived from content published there under the **GNU Free Documentation License, version
  1.2**. Verified 2026-08-07 against the wiki's own footer as served on its front page ("Content
  is available under GNU Free Documentation License 1.2 unless otherwise noted"); the wiki's
  General disclaimer page is empty, so the footer is the sole site-wide notice, and the same
  "unless otherwise noted" per-page caveat and intake check apply as for the Zandronum Wiki
  below. Redistribution/modification of that material must comply with GFDL 1.2's terms. A verbatim copy of the license text is included in this repo at
  [licenses/GFDL-1.2.txt](licenses/GFDL-1.2.txt) per GFDL 1.2's own requirement that copies of
  licensed material be accompanied by the license text.
- Files whose `Provenance:` cites the Zandronum Wiki (`wiki.zandronum.com`, whether by page title
  or URL) are derived from content published there under **Creative Commons
  Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)**. Verified 2026-08-07
  against the wiki's own footer ("Content is available under Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike
  4.0 International unless otherwise noted") and its `ZandronumWiki:General_disclaimer` page
  (https://wiki.zandronum.com/w/index.php?title=ZandronumWiki:General_disclaimer&oldid=1919),
  which reaffirms that license as the sole terms governing use and modification of the wiki's
  content. The footer's "unless otherwise noted" leaves room for an individual page to carry
  different terms - the intake process checks each saved page for a per-page license notice
  (none found to date; see `maintainer/PROCESS_INTAKE_FILE.md`). **Note the NonCommercial
  clause** — this restricts commercial reuse of that subset of files beyond what the MIT grant in
  section 1 would otherwise allow, independent of how this repo itself is licensed. Full legal
  text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode

Every wiki-derived file's `**Provenance:**` field is the authoritative record of which of the two
source licenses applies to that specific file; check it before reusing such a file outside this
repo.

Accordingly, each wiki-derived file is licensed **as a whole - including this repo's own
contributions to it -** under its source wiki's license: GFDL 1.2 for ZDoom-Wiki-derived files,
CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 for Zandronum-Wiki-derived files. This is what those licenses' modified-version
(GFDL 1.2 §4) and ShareAlike (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 §3(b)) terms require of shared adaptations, and it
is the affirmative grant for the rewritten prose in those files - the MIT grant in section 1
never applies to them. Every wiki-derived entry here is substantially rewritten, restructured,
and re-verified against engine/compiler source relative to the wiki revision its `Provenance:`
cites: treat all wiki-derived material in this tree as modified from its source, never as a
verbatim mirror. As with section 1, this whole-file grant covers the file's prose, structure,
and analysis - not any verbatim engine/compiler source excerpt sitting inside it, which remains
under its own section 3 or 4 terms (permissive, which is why it can lawfully sit inside a
GFDL/CC-licensed file at all; check for a `**Source excerpt:**` field). Downstream reusers who copy a wiki-derived `.md` file out of this tree should
carry this section (or an equivalent notice) along with it, the same way section 3 asks for
excerpt-bearing files - and, for a GFDL file, a copy of
[licenses/GFDL-1.2.txt](licenses/GFDL-1.2.txt) as well.

**No single file may derive from both wikis.** GFDL 1.2 carries no "or any later version" clause
and CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 is a different copyleft with a NonCommercial term on top; the two cannot both
be satisfied at once, so a file drawing on both would be unlicensable and no section here could
rescue it. Where one topic needs material from both, it is split into one file per wiki, each
citing only its own. `tools/lint_docs.py` rejects a `**Provenance:**` field naming both, and
`shared/AUTHORING.md`'s "Wiki provenance" states the rule for authors.

3. Verbatim Zandronum engine source excerpts
-----------------------------------------------

A number of doc files, to precisely document observed behavior, quote short excerpts (a function
body, a `case` block, a handful of related lines) directly from the Zandronum engine's own C++
source — not from a wiki, and not paraphrased. Any such file carries a
`**Source excerpt:** Zandronum engine source ...` field.

The specific Zandronum source files these excerpts are drawn from (`src/p_acs.cpp`,
`src/p_lnspec.cpp`, `src/team.cpp`, and similar) are themselves released under a permissive
BSD-3-Clause-style license (verified directly against the file headers in the Zandronum source,
not just its top-level `LICENSE.txt`, since Zandronum's own license notes that "some source files
contain additional notices of original copyright by their contributors"):

    Copyright 1998-2012 Randy Heit; Copyright (C) 2002 Brad Carney; Copyright (C) 2007-2012
    Skulltag Development Team; Copyright (c) 2012, Zandronum Development Team. All rights reserved.

    Redistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without modification, are permitted
    provided that the following conditions are met:

    1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright notice, this list of
       conditions and the following disclaimer.
    2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright notice, this list of
       conditions and the following disclaimer in the documentation and/or other materials
       provided with the distribution.
    3. The name(s) of the author(s)/team may not be used to endorse or promote products derived
       from this software without specific prior written permission.
    4. Redistributions in any form must be accompanied by information on how to obtain complete
       source code for the software and any accompanying software that uses the software. The
       source code must either be included in the distribution or be available for no more than
       the cost of distribution plus a nominal fee, and must be freely redistributable under
       reasonable conditions.

    THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS" AND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES ARE DISCLAIMED. IN NO
    EVENT SHALL THE COPYRIGHT HOLDER OR CONTRIBUTORS BE LIABLE FOR ANY DAMAGES ARISING IN ANY WAY
    OUT OF THE USE OF THIS SOFTWARE.

This notice, plus each excerpt's own file:line citation in its doc's `**Provenance:**` field,
satisfies condition 1 (notice retained) for the whole repo at once. Condition 4 (information on
how to obtain the complete source) is satisfied by this pointer: the complete Zandronum source is
freely available at https://github.com/TorrSamaho/zandronum. Downstream reusers who copy a single
excerpt-bearing `.md` file out of this tree should carry this section (or an equivalent pointer to
it) along with it to stay compliant.

This is a different, and more permissive, license than either wiki's — it is not copyleft and does
not restrict commercial use, so it doesn't conflict with this repo's own MIT terms in section 1
beyond the attribution/source-pointer obligation described above.

4. Verbatim zt-bcc/bcc compiler source excerpts
---------------------------------------------------

`zt-bcc` (and the upstream `bcc` project it forks, `positively-charged/bcc`) is MIT-licensed,
Copyright (c) 2013 Daniel Baimiachkine — the same permissive terms as this repo's own original
material in section 1. Reusing such an excerpt would only require keeping this notice; there is no
copyleft or commercial-use restriction to track separately.

A note on engine source this repo does NOT quote
-------------------------------------------------

UZDoom/GZDoom engine source and the ZScript standard library (`wadsrc/static/zscript/`) are
GPL-3.0. Unlike `acc` — also excluded from verbatim quoting, but only for lack of a permissive
fallback — GPL-3.0 material would be perfectly quotable *if* this repo were prepared to carry
GPL-3.0 obligations for the files that did it. It deliberately isn't: `shared/AUTHORING.md`'s
"Quoting engine/compiler source verbatim" makes paraphrase-only a hard rule with no excerpt
exception, precisely so no file in this tree ever needs a section 5. If you ever find a fenced
code block that looks like real UZDoom/GZDoom C++ or ZScript stdlib source, treat it as a bug in
the doc file, not as material this section failed to license — `tools/lint_docs.py` treats it as
a hard lint error for the same reason.

5. Practical guidance for reusing this repo
----------------------------------------------

- If you only need signature/parameter lookups, or a file with no `**Source excerpt:**` field and
  a non-wiki `Provenance:`, section 1's MIT terms cover you outright.
- If a file's `Provenance:` cites a wiki page, check section 2 and comply with the corresponding
  wiki's license — in particular, verify NonCommercial compliance before using a
  Zandronum-Wiki-derived (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0) file in a commercial context, and carry section 2's
  notice (plus, for a GFDL file, [licenses/GFDL-1.2.txt](licenses/GFDL-1.2.txt)) along with any
  copy you make.
- If a file carries a `**Source excerpt:**` field, comply with section 3 or 4 as indicated — in
  practice this just means keeping this LICENSE file (or the relevant section of it) alongside any
  copy you make, since neither excerpt source imposes copyleft or commercial restrictions.
- A single file can be subject to more than one of the above at once (e.g. a tier-A,
  Zandronum-Wiki-derived file that also quotes engine source) — check every applicable field before
  assuming only one section applies. The one combination that never occurs is both of section 2's
  wikis in the same file; see that section for why it can't.
